From: Trijal Saha <97483939+Trijal08@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 5 Nov 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] samsung-sit: retire the boot-parity replay for boot config

mm-sit-boot-parity.h was a generated transcript of one captured Android
boot: 246 rows of opaque blobs, including the same request twenty-four
times because the capture window caught a poll loop, and values that were
true only for the two SIMs in the device it was taken from. It is replaced
by 48 named requests, each justified.

Every payload was checked against five Android captures spanning two
baseband builds, two carriers, both stacks, warm and cold boots. 34 of the
42 message ids are byte-identical in all of them, so they are constants
here and are still right on another SIM in another country. The rest are
derived:

  SET_BROADCAST_SMS_CONFIG  built from the 3GPP TS 23.041 channel table
  SET_OPEN_CARRIER_INFO     carries this SIM's MCCMNC
  SET_MOBILE_DATA_STATE     carries the logical stack
  AIMS_SET_CALL_WAITING     carries the current time

Cell broadcast was the one row that would have been wrong elsewhere. It is
not national policy, as it first looked: stock enables every identifier
that carries a real alert and disables every test and exercise channel,
which is the standard's own distinction and means the same thing in every
country. Built from that table it reproduces stock's 57 bytes exactly, and
a new test pins it. Erring towards enabling is deliberate -- a channel the
local network never uses costs nothing, a disabled one is an alert the user
does not get.

Dropped, with reasons:

  14 pure getters, whose responses were discarded. A getter cannot
     configure anything.
  22 AIMS rows in the RADIO phase. mm-sit-ims.c builds and sends that block
     itself, per session and properly stack-indexed, within a second of the
     context request; these were the same block sent 20-25s too early.

Two rows were wrong and are fixed. SET_FAST_DORMANCY_INFO and
SIT_SET_GPS_SUPL_NI_READY were marked stack-1-only, an artifact of the
single boot the table was generated from; stock sends both on both stacks
in all five captures. Conversely SEND_SGC_VALUE, SET_DEBUG_TRACE and 0x1307
really are stack-0-only in all five, so that is now stated rather than
implied.

The PRE_PDN block stays a verbatim replay and says so. Its purpose is not
its settings but that the CP's last impression before a context request
matches stock's, so it cannot be half-converted. The comment records what
would retire it: a boot without it that still gets both stacks granted on
the first ask. Since r60 that is 6 for 6, so the experiment is now cheap.
